  "name": "ANTI SACHHAROMYCES CEREVISIAE - IgA",
  "code": "2681",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"83\" data-end=\"340\"><strong data-start=\"83\" data-end=\"95\">ASCA IgA</strong> is an antibody test used primarily in the <strong data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"208\">diagnosis and differentiation of inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD)</strong>, particularly <strong data-start=\"223\" data-end=\"242\">Crohn&rsquo;s disease</strong>. The test detects <strong data-start=\"261\" data-end=\"279\">IgA antibodies</strong> directed against <em data-start=\"297\" data-end=\"323\">Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em> (baker&rsquo;s yeast).</p>\n<p data-start=\"1273\" data-end=\"1302\"><strong data-start=\"1277\" data-end=\"1302\">Clinical Significance</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1304\" data-end=\"1699\">\n<li data-start=\"1304\" data-end=\"1559\">\n<p data-start=\"1306\" data-end=\"1336\"><strong data-start=\"1306\" data-end=\"1335\">Positive ASCA IgA and IgG</strong>:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1339\" data-end=\"1559\">\n<li data-start=\"1339\" data-end=\"1388\">\n<p data-start=\"1341\" data-end=\"1388\">Seen in <strong data-start=\"1349\" data-end=\"1359\">40&ndash;70%</strong> of Crohn&rsquo;s disease patients.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1391\" data-end=\"1436\">\n<p data-start=\"1393\" data-end=\"1436\">Rarely seen in ulcerative colitis patients.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1439\" data-end=\"1559\">\n<p data-start=\"1441\" data-end=\"1559\">Not diagnostic alone, but <strong data-start=\"1467\" data-end=\"1505\">supports Crohn's disease diagnosis</strong> when used alongside clinical and endoscopic findings.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1561\" data-end=\"1621\">\n<p data-start=\"1563\" data-end=\"1621\"><strong data-start=\"1563\" data-end=\"1580\">ASCA-negative</strong> patients can still have Crohn&rsquo;s disease.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1622\" data-end=\"1699\">\n<p data-start=\"1624\" data-end=\"1699\"><strong data-start=\"1624\" data-end=\"1643\">False positives</strong> can occur in some infections and autoimmune conditions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Test Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Positive ASCA IgA</strong></td>\n<td>Suggestive of <strong>Crohn&rsquo;s disease</strong>, especially when combined with positive ASCA IgG and negative pANCA. Supports immune reactivity to yeast antigens.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negative ASCA IgA</strong></td>\n<td>Does <strong>not rule out Crohn&rsquo;s disease</strong>. May be seen in ulcerative colitis, non-IBD conditions, or healthy individuals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Borderline/Equivocal</strong></td>\n<td>Inconclusive; may need repeat testing or further evaluation based on symptoms.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"374\">&nbsp;</h3>",
